WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:07.860
- Welcoming for a fireside chat,
Christopher Bishop and Harry Shum.

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:07.980
- Great.

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:18.620
Harry first of all, thanks hugely for taking
the time to join us.

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:21.970
- Of course.
- I know you've had an incredibly busy day in just,

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:24.600
I guess you've literally
just flown back in to Seattle so.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.080
- Yeah, you're right.

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:30.980
Well first of all, congratulations Chris both
for the incredible lab

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:34.000
you have built over
20 years in Cambridge,

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.490
and of course,

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:38.370
nothing beats this great honor
of the Royal Society,

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:40.080
signing on the same book as Newton.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:42.130
It's an incredible achievement.

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:44.940
I just came back about two hours ago.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:51.250
I was this morning at this big Microsoft
internal event we call Ready.

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:54.780
This is actually a week-long
event for our sales force.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:01:01.230
Half of our company, roughly half of our company
is actually working on the sales and marketing side.

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:05.730
Every year we use this opportunity
to share with our field,

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:07.940
the sales force what's happening in the company,

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:10.660
and we also live broadcast

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:14.420
all those presentations
from the conference

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:15.850
and across the company.

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:17.420
It happens to be in Vegas.

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:19.280
It's only 114 degrees there.

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:21.180
It's just incredible.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:23.220
I hardly went outside.

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:27.190
So I was so happy to come back to join
you and the Faculty Summit.

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:28.320
- That's great you could be here.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:33.700
So I thought we would have maybe 20 minutes or so,
you and I have a bit of chat about AI,

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:36.170
and then I'll open it up to the audience.

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:40.960
So please, be thinking of some interesting questions
on the topic of AI for Harry.

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:44.700
Maybe I can just dive right in and I'm going
to start with a fairly tough question.

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:45.450
But maybe I can just dive right in.

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:46.680
And I'm going to start
with a fairly tough question.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:50.130
This is a bit like asking parents
about their favorite child or something.

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:54.720
I mean, there's a lot of work in
MSR worldwide around AI, but are there any things

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:56.740
that particularly stand out?

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:57.830
- That's really a tough one.

00:01:57.830 --> 00:02:00.940
And I guess if I can pick just one,
the rest will be easy.

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:08.150
There's just so many exciting things going on in
MSR, and I feel extremely fortunate and very proud

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:10.860
of what we have done in the last 26 years.

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:19.710
In MSR there are actually three areas
I recently got very excited about it,

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:24.130
and not surprisingly, you would agree
that AI is certainly one of them.

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:25.610
I will get to that later.

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:31.170
Two other areas I feel very, very excited about,
and one is actually a very forward looking,

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:34.300
and the other one is also very timely.

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:37.000
The one very forward
looking is quantum computing.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:42.060
We have been working on the Station
Q, quantum computing for a long, long time.

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:48.710
I think we're getting to a point that we feel
we're getting very close

00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:52.350
to get the stable qubits that we can produce.

00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:57.090
Certainly, you know Mike Friedman will be able
to tell you a lot more details.

00:02:57.090 --> 00:03:01.240
One thing I can share with you
is that we feel so confident

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:03.340
that we actually started
to really work

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:05.030
with experimental physicists

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:06.600
to really get to the physics level,

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:09.250
how can we construct those qubits?

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:13.020
Once you get enough of those qubits,
then great things can happen.

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:18.590
The other area I feel very, very excited about,
and they're very timely for Microsoft

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:21.270
is on security and the privacy.

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:27.080
Specifically on security, this incredible initiative
across all our labs, certainly you know,

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:30.080
Cambridge Lab as well as on this path,

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:33.350
what we call the confidential
computing and the secure computing.

00:03:33.850 --> 00:03:40.210
And the idea there is really that, you know,
not only can do encryption at rest and on the go,

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:42.120
but also during computation.

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:47.280
So this has been a very exciting research area
for us over quite a few years.

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:52.880
It's fantastic to see we have an opportunity
to move that technology into Microsoft products.

00:03:54.130 --> 00:03:56.620
Certainly I want
- That's very relevant for AI, of course.

00:03:56.620 --> 00:03:58.200
- Absolutely.
- But also data driven and we care

00:03:58.200 --> 00:03:59.850
about the privacy and security.

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:05.770
- Yeah, it has profound impact over many,
many things that we want to do at Microsoft.

00:04:05.770 --> 00:04:08.830
And the AI, of course,
there are many things that we do.

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:12.970
I heard Eric give a fantastic
presentation this morning,

00:04:12.970 --> 00:04:14.480
and I had the opportunity

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:16.950
to go through his slides earlier.

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:22.630
We do many things, and I'm not going
to talk about a lot of things

00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:23.880
that Eric mentioned about it,

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:26.030
I will just bring a couple of examples

00:04:26.030 --> 00:04:28.500
of where I see great

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:32.430
MSR research having incredible
impact on the products.

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:35.640
You know, one of those areas
is actually machine reading.

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:39.560
A lot of interesting things
happening in natural language,

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:42.720
and that we as a company deal
with a lot of text,

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:44.620
whether it's email, web page,

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:46.950
or even any kind of documents.

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:51.660
There's so much that we can do,
we absolutely are working hard on that.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:05:00.160
Office 365, Outlook, Word and many
of those things will depend on a lot of

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:02.560
MSR technology along this line

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:05.560
we loosely call the machine
reading comprehension.

00:05:06.510 --> 00:05:10.400
The other areas actually are probably
a little bit of personal bias

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:14.750
is really the connection
between the visual images,

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:17.970
videos and natural
language description.

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:22.680
Sometimes call them captions;
captions from image, captions from video.

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:29.230
I think true image understanding
has to be connected with natural language.

00:05:29.230 --> 00:05:32.000
- And you're giving a keynote,
aren't you, next week is it?

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:34.270
- Well actually, this Sunday.
- This Sunday.

00:05:34.270 --> 00:05:35.420
- It's a little bit embarrassing.

00:05:35.420 --> 00:05:43.690
I was invited to go give keynotes at the CVPR,
which used to be my main conference until 2007.

00:05:43.690 --> 00:05:48.610
I believe 2007 was the last time
I really participated at the CVPR.

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:53.780
This time I got a chance to go
attend again and they gave a keynote.

00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:59.520
And they are mostly really about sharing
some lessons that we have learned from Microsoft.

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:03.620
The title of my talk is
called Commercializing Computer Vision.

00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:07.460
So I will talk about a few examples,
including HoloLens,

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:10.520
how we actually organize research efforts,

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:13.180
how we actually do move the research

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:16.310
to the product side
and with the flywheel.

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:17.070
- That's great.

00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:19.400
Actually, that's a good hook into my next question,

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:21.800
which is, sort of step back a little bit

00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:23.600
and a few months ago,

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:27.110
the company created this new organization, AI&amp;R,

00:06:27.110 --> 00:06:28.970
and appointed you
as the leader of this.

00:06:28.970 --> 00:06:32.880
So can you tell us a little bit
about AI&amp;R and its mission?

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:33.520
- Yeah.

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:40.970
Well, I am incredibly excited about and very honored
to have this opportunity to lead to AI&amp;R for Microsoft.

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:47.900
AI&amp;R is actually our force
engineering division and the research division.

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:51.050
- And the R is research.
- R is research, AI and the research.

00:06:51.770 --> 00:06:56.060
The other three parts of engineering groups,
in case you don't know and are curious,

00:06:56.060 --> 00:06:58.060
those three are Windows

00:06:58.060 --> 00:07:01.370
and devices, Office productivity

00:07:01.370 --> 00:07:03.920
and clouds and enterprise.

00:07:04.470 --> 00:07:09.150
AI and the research is the fourth one,
and the latest and the smallest one, actually.

00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:11.750
We only have 7,500 people in our division.

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:19.620
But it's very timely and important
for the company as you see the incredible progress

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:22.220
that has been made in the last several years,

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:23.560
in particular in AI.

00:07:24.070 --> 00:07:28.790
Many areas of AI, many of those
great examples this morning

00:07:28.790 --> 00:07:32.160
from Eric you've already seen,

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:35.570
but we feel this is really the right time

00:07:35.570 --> 00:07:38.750
to not only do the greater research,

00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:42.950
but also really having impacts
on Microsoft business.

00:07:42.950 --> 00:07:46.780
First of all through products
and the services and the opportunities

00:07:46.780 --> 00:07:49.190
to really democratize AI technology

00:07:49.190 --> 00:07:51.120
to third parties
to our customers.

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:53.480
So that is then
the really starting point.

00:07:53.480 --> 00:07:58.240
And recently I have had
a lot of opportunities talking

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:00.250
to many people
outside of Microsoft

00:08:00.250 --> 00:08:01.820
as well,
including many CEOs,

00:08:01.820 --> 00:08:04.570
CTOs, and the
engineering leaders.

00:08:04.570 --> 00:08:11.210
Everyone's just so excited about AI,
and also so worried about, you know,

00:08:11.210 --> 00:08:12.960
what if we miss this AI thing

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:15.360
I think there's certainly
a lot of hype over there.

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:21.110
My joke is that you know, you have
to claim that you like and that you do AI,

00:08:21.110 --> 00:08:22.560
artificial intelligence,

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:25.130
because the opposite
of artificial intelligence

00:08:25.130 --> 00:08:27.030
is natural stupidity.

00:08:27.030 --> 00:08:29.400
So you just have to say it.

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:31.710
You know, if you don't do AI,
what else did you do?

00:08:31.710 --> 00:08:32.670
So that's a problem.

00:08:32.670 --> 00:08:36.380
And but seriously,
on a more serious note,

00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:39.510
we really believe there are great
opportunities for the company

00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:41.870
and for our customers,
even more importantly.

00:08:41.870 --> 00:08:42.720
- And anything in particular?

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:45.660
Any particular products or services
that you see coming along?

00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:47.910
- Yeah, I'd be happy to share more on that.

00:08:47.910 --> 00:08:52.380
So the way we think about with the AI
and the research division

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:55.400
is really about what kind of products

00:08:55.400 --> 00:08:57.440
and services that we can offer.

00:08:57.440 --> 00:09:02.340
So by and large, we think about those
three lines of products that we built.

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:05.900
The first one is really
the first party AI products

00:09:05.900 --> 00:09:09.570
that probably few know, reasonable.

00:09:09.570 --> 00:09:15.380
Along the line of say, search engine
Bing and the digital assistants like Cortana.

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:21.330
I certainly very much believe
in that, that ultimate form of AI

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:24.890
is actually in digital assistants,

00:09:24.890 --> 00:09:28.020
in the right place, right time
with the right information,

00:09:29.230 --> 00:09:31.610
helping you to do right things.

00:09:32.270 --> 00:09:33.520
It's still very, very early.

00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:38.320
Along that line we actually do
very good work on chat bots.

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:43.810
So we have been building chat bots
like no one else, and especially we start from China.

00:09:44.780 --> 00:09:48.770
Great success, then we were
doing Rena in Japan,

00:09:48.770 --> 00:09:51.200
Zoe in the United States,

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:54.210
and we did Raul in India.

00:09:54.210 --> 00:09:56.260
So we continue to expand our effort.

00:09:56.260 --> 00:10:01.560
So this is one line of products
that we feel very committed in the long term

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:03.280
and will keep pushing.

00:10:03.280 --> 00:10:07.980
The second line of products,
also very reasonable for Microsoft

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:11.150
taking on is what we called infusing AI

00:10:11.150 --> 00:10:13.150
into every single product

00:10:13.150 --> 00:10:15.570
and service Microsoft offers,

00:10:15.570 --> 00:10:17.570
especially with Office.

00:10:17.570 --> 00:10:21.420
Imagine that with PowerPoint, you know,
we already embedded

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:24.080
in a machine translation
to PowerPoint,

00:10:24.080 --> 00:10:26.890
you know, when you do
PowerPoint presentations, the audience,

00:10:26.890 --> 00:10:30.810
depending on what kind
of languages they like,

00:10:30.810 --> 00:10:33.550
they can tune in to that language.

00:10:33.550 --> 00:10:38.970
Another thing we actually incorporated into PowerPoint
is actually image caching.

00:10:38.970 --> 00:10:42.090
So now when you import
an image into PowerPoint, you know,

00:10:42.090 --> 00:10:44.210
immediately we can
offer you image

00:10:44.210 --> 00:10:46.200
captions if we feel confident

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:49.220
If we are not confident
after analyzing the picture,

00:10:49.220 --> 00:10:50.940
we might be confident enough

00:10:50.940 --> 00:10:53.590
to show you some entities
detected from the image.

00:10:53.590 --> 00:10:55.810
So it's still very early.

00:10:55.810 --> 00:10:57.810
But with PowerPoint,
we actually every day

00:10:57.810 --> 00:11:00.370
we actually have
10 million images

00:11:00.370 --> 00:11:02.370
that actually get captioned.

00:11:03.230 --> 00:11:07.740
And we also have Word, PowerPoint and
many other things that we can do.

00:11:07.740 --> 00:11:11.590
The great work from your lab,
for example, with the Excel,

00:11:11.590 --> 00:11:14.780
what we are doing
as you import data

00:11:14.780 --> 00:11:16.300
into various cells,

00:11:16.300 --> 00:11:18.240
you know, what kind of things
we can process there

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:20.210
using some machine
learning and AI tech.

00:11:20.210 --> 00:11:23.720
So that's really second line
of work that we do.

00:11:23.720 --> 00:11:28.580
And I should also mention about
really applying AI into our cloud.

00:11:29.190 --> 00:11:35.470
Azure is becoming the most intelligent cloud,
literally, if you look at what kind of AI

00:11:35.470 --> 00:11:37.170
capabilities we can offer there.

00:11:38.030 --> 00:11:42.820
The third line of products
that I would say is pretty new

00:11:42.820 --> 00:11:45.330
and is something

00:11:45.330 --> 00:11:47.740
that I have thought
of long and hard,

00:11:47.740 --> 00:11:49.670
and have decided that we have to take that

00:11:49.670 --> 00:11:51.240
on is really the new

00:11:51.240 --> 00:11:53.650
and the emerging
business processes.

00:11:55.470 --> 00:11:58.720
As we think about all different
kinds of verticals

00:11:58.720 --> 00:12:00.790
and all different
vertical industries

00:12:00.790 --> 00:12:04.290
and all different kind of
horizontal business processes,

00:12:04.290 --> 00:12:08.100
I certainly believe that they will
all be disrupted by AI and the data.

00:12:08.670 --> 00:12:13.490
That the vertical industries
like health, health care, something you,

00:12:13.490 --> 00:12:16.860
Peter and Chris Jones
have been working on,

00:12:18.420 --> 00:12:20.570
it's just so obvious
that we should get in

00:12:20.570 --> 00:12:23.590
and apply our AI technology
to challenges

00:12:24.680 --> 00:12:25.960
and with what business model

00:12:25.960 --> 00:12:28.370
and how should we think
about a partnership?

00:12:29.210 --> 00:12:33.330
And as we think about the horizontals,
it is maybe even more interesting.

00:12:33.330 --> 00:12:37.360
In all the business processes,
so we actually deal with today

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:38.990
like sales and marketing,

00:12:38.990 --> 00:12:41.410
like customer support
and customer services,

00:12:41.410 --> 00:12:43.280
like HR and recruiting,

00:12:43.280 --> 00:12:46.170
all those things
will be transformed.

00:12:46.170 --> 00:12:49.390
It's just so obvious, so obvious,
it should be in everyone's mind.

00:12:49.390 --> 00:12:50.470
Now you actually have the data.

00:12:50.470 --> 00:12:53.160
Now we actually can do the process,
now you can apply AI.

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:58.680
So those things, in my mind,
would be low-hanging fruits, so to speak,

00:12:58.680 --> 00:13:03.070
if you care about
creating a new business.

00:13:03.070 --> 00:13:08.590
So those three directions are what
we are now focused on in Microsoft,

00:13:08.590 --> 00:13:10.690
especially for AI&amp;R.

00:13:10.690 --> 00:13:12.380
But it's really not just us.

00:13:12.380 --> 00:13:15.490
We work together with other parts of the company.

00:13:15.490 --> 00:13:16.180
- That's great.

00:13:16.180 --> 00:13:17.580
An amazing time to be in the field.

00:13:17.580 --> 00:13:18.910
- Absolutely.
- There's so much happening.

00:13:18.910 --> 00:13:20.960
- Absolutely.
- Let me ask you a little bit

00:13:20.960 --> 00:13:23.200
about Microsoft in particular.

00:13:23.200 --> 00:13:28.770
How would you say that Microsoft's approach to AI
perhaps differs from those of others?

00:13:28.770 --> 00:13:30.570
What makes Microsoft special when it comes to AI?

00:13:31.900 --> 00:13:37.090
- Well Chris, I would start by asking
a slightly more general question.

00:13:37.090 --> 00:13:39.890
What is special about Microsoft?

00:13:39.890 --> 00:13:46.830
I think every company has its own culture,
has its own mission, has its own point of view.

00:13:46.830 --> 00:13:48.490
You know, why should we exist?

00:13:48.490 --> 00:13:53.020
You know, Satya was very clear that
we should always question ourselves.

00:13:53.020 --> 00:13:56.920
What would people miss if we simply
just disappear as a company?

00:13:57.540 --> 00:14:03.580
I think we are very, very clear that we exist
to serve other people in other companies.

00:14:03.580 --> 00:14:07.950
Our company mission statement
is incredibly crisp, in my opinion.

00:14:07.950 --> 00:14:15.450
That's empower every person and every organization
on the planet to achieve more, now with AI.

00:14:15.450 --> 00:14:18.150
So that is really the high level,
guiding principle.

00:14:18.150 --> 00:14:19.900
So do that with AI.

00:14:20.750 --> 00:14:26.050
And more specifically, I would say
if you look at our position, our assets,

00:14:26.590 --> 00:14:27.850
our history,

00:14:27.850 --> 00:14:29.940
I think we approach AI

00:14:31.860 --> 00:14:33.890
with a very practical approach,

00:14:33.890 --> 00:14:36.750
yet with long-term commitment.

00:14:36.750 --> 00:14:38.550
I start with long-term commitment.

00:14:38.550 --> 00:14:41.450
So we started Microsoft
Research 26 years ago.

00:14:42.390 --> 00:14:45.970
At that time, you know, Bill Gates said,
"Wow, wouldn't it be nice, you know,

00:14:45.970 --> 00:14:49.110
someday computers could see,

00:14:49.110 --> 00:14:52.500
hear, talk and understand
human beings."

00:14:52.500 --> 00:14:54.100
What is that?

00:14:54.100 --> 00:14:55.960
Those computers are called AIs.

00:14:55.960 --> 00:15:02.310
So most people don't know that among
the first groups we ever started in the Redmond Lab,

00:15:02.310 --> 00:15:04.140
that's the first lab we ever started.

00:15:04.930 --> 00:15:10.260
The first three groups were the natural language
group, speech group and the computer vision group.

00:15:10.260 --> 00:15:13.910
So we started that 26 years ago,

00:15:13.910 --> 00:15:18.130
anticipating AI would make great
progress in the general AI,

00:15:18.130 --> 00:15:20.270
and work towards general AI.

00:15:21.460 --> 00:15:25.110
We definitely continue to have
this commitment and the confidence.

00:15:25.110 --> 00:15:31.650
We will continue to invest in research,
really take on those very tough problems.

00:15:31.650 --> 00:15:34.580
The question that you always want
to address in that area,

00:15:34.580 --> 00:15:37.120
that you and many others, care about

00:15:37.120 --> 00:15:38.370
and we will continue
to push that.

00:15:39.400 --> 00:15:43.920
Yet at the same time, I would say,
we do have those products

00:15:43.920 --> 00:15:45.810
that can infuse AI

00:15:45.810 --> 00:15:47.740
to immediately help
our customers.

00:15:47.740 --> 00:15:55.570
I already mentioned about PowerPoint,
I already mentioned about cloud and also Excel,

00:15:55.570 --> 00:15:59.000
you can imagine that Word
would become more intelligent.

00:15:59.000 --> 00:16:03.780
Word should become a reading
and writing tool much more intelligent than today.

00:16:04.720 --> 00:16:07.410
Even more interesting to me,
you know, my favorite application

00:16:07.410 --> 00:16:09.330
nowadays of AI
is actually Outlook.

00:16:09.910 --> 00:16:13.220
There's really no reason why
Outlook search is so difficult,

00:16:13.220 --> 00:16:15.560
and a smart reply
is not very smart.

00:16:15.560 --> 00:16:17.470
Reminders are not very intelligent.

00:16:17.470 --> 00:16:21.560
All those things now,
we AI&amp;R work together

00:16:21.560 --> 00:16:23.970
with Office teams very,
very closely.

00:16:23.970 --> 00:16:29.390
So I would say that first of all
we have our own company's mission

00:16:29.390 --> 00:16:32.450
and that really guides our AI work,

00:16:32.450 --> 00:16:35.080
and then we are both practical
in the long term.

00:16:35.080 --> 00:16:35.760
- That sounds great.

00:16:35.760 --> 00:16:38.610
So lots of opportunities then for AI.

00:16:38.610 --> 00:16:40.480
What about the other side?

00:16:40.480 --> 00:16:42.080
What about the risks associated?

00:16:42.080 --> 00:16:45.400
I mean do you wake up at 3 am worried
about killer robots and lasers?

00:16:46.780 --> 00:16:48.690
Do you see any risks in AI?

00:16:48.690 --> 00:16:49.970
- I do.

00:16:49.970 --> 00:16:54.020
I don't worry about those products, yeah.

00:16:54.560 --> 00:16:57.890
There are many things
that we should worry about,

00:16:57.890 --> 00:17:04.490
But I'm not too worried about
things like super intelligence and AI,

00:17:04.490 --> 00:17:06.460
but there are practical things

00:17:06.460 --> 00:17:08.560
that we do need to worry
about in my opinion.

00:17:08.560 --> 00:17:15.660
The first thing I would say, probably is
whatever AI applied to the physical world,

00:17:16.360 --> 00:17:19.800
a concrete example
is actually self-driving cars.

00:17:22.210 --> 00:17:30.890
I would predict that the most challenging time would be
when half of those cars on the street are self-driving,

00:17:30.890 --> 00:17:32.680
and half of them
are still human driving.

00:17:33.240 --> 00:17:35.260
I don't think we really understand enough yet.

00:17:35.260 --> 00:17:40.400
I think we are fooling ourselves, just so you know,
today, autonomous driving is already there.

00:17:42.410 --> 00:17:44.100
It's just going to take some time.

00:17:45.310 --> 00:17:50.610
I learned something just a couple of weeks ago
at the MIT advisory board meeting.

00:17:50.610 --> 00:17:56.470
Rodney Brooks was there talking about the robotics
and inevitably one of the topics

00:17:56.470 --> 00:17:58.380
there was actually self-driving.

00:17:59.030 --> 00:18:04.390
And he defined the standard
in his point of view, he's actually very cautious about

00:18:04.390 --> 00:18:07.370
how soon we would see autonomous
driving cars there.

00:18:07.890 --> 00:18:10.280
His counter-argument is straightforward.

00:18:10.280 --> 00:18:14.750
He said look, if you drive in Cambridge, Boston.

00:18:14.750 --> 00:18:16.560
The fake Cambridge, you know,
not the real one.

00:18:16.560 --> 00:18:22.680
So if you're driving in Cambridge,
you really couldn't drive

00:18:22.680 --> 00:18:25.730
if you don't violate
the traffic laws.

00:18:25.730 --> 00:18:29.100
Now how do you program into that?

00:18:29.760 --> 00:18:32.850
And I think that is something
that we're still learning.

00:18:32.850 --> 00:18:35.630
It's still very, very early
and the direction is very clear.

00:18:35.630 --> 00:18:37.490
It's just about the staging.

00:18:37.490 --> 00:18:40.020
The other thing, you know, we as a company,

00:18:40.020 --> 00:18:41.740
as an industry worry

00:18:41.740 --> 00:18:45.770
about is the profound impact with AI,

00:18:45.770 --> 00:18:47.640
with automation,

00:18:47.640 --> 00:18:50.300
that we will see on the job market.

00:18:50.860 --> 00:18:57.390
I think every technology disruption,
every technology innovation, we have certain impacts.

00:18:57.390 --> 00:19:01.670
But this time I think people
tend to believe this thing

00:19:01.670 --> 00:19:05.140
is probably going to be
more real than others.

00:19:05.140 --> 00:19:09.240
We don't understand what kind of new jobs
it will open up.

00:19:09.240 --> 00:19:14.440
We already see some jobs
will be transformed.

00:19:14.440 --> 00:19:19.010
So those are the things that actually
we worry about as a company.

00:19:19.010 --> 00:19:25.730
And maybe the last thing I personally, you know,
worry about AI, not much about super intelligence,

00:19:25.730 --> 00:19:28.060
is really that the reason AI

00:19:28.060 --> 00:19:31.400
is so different from many

00:19:31.400 --> 00:19:33.570
other incredible innovations

00:19:33.570 --> 00:19:35.540
in the history of human being,

00:19:35.540 --> 00:19:38.070
is that most of
the previous innovations,

00:19:38.070 --> 00:19:43.100
there's much about extending
human physical capability.

00:19:43.100 --> 00:19:47.570
And this time with AI, we're talking
about extending brain powers as well.

00:19:48.200 --> 00:19:50.830
Yet, we don't really
understand the human brains.

00:19:50.830 --> 00:19:55.280
And the neuroscience is still, in my opinion,
still very much early stage.

00:19:55.280 --> 00:19:57.970
So something that we will keep learning.

00:19:58.780 --> 00:19:59.750
- Yeah, that's very interesting.

00:19:59.750 --> 00:20:02.540
I think actually also there's a risk the other way,

00:20:02.540 --> 00:20:04.800
there's a risk of things
that may not go as well as we hope.

00:20:04.800 --> 00:20:11.390
But I wonder also that with very justifiable concerns
about the danger about privacy and security

00:20:11.390 --> 00:20:14.680
and so on, that we might go too far
in the other direction,

00:20:14.680 --> 00:20:15.990
and then actually,
I mean, take for instance

00:20:15.990 --> 00:20:17.360
in the health care space,
for instance.

00:20:17.360 --> 00:20:22.020
There's tremendous societal benefit
that we can see is potential

00:20:22.020 --> 00:20:23.330
that is latent there in AI,

00:20:23.330 --> 00:20:27.230
and we may shut that out
if we overreact to some of these risks.

00:20:27.230 --> 00:20:29.790
So it feels like we need to find
the right sort of middle ground here.

00:20:29.790 --> 00:20:30.550
- Right.

00:20:30.550 --> 00:20:36.710
I think that one small step toward that,
and first of all, I totally agree with you on that.

00:20:37.450 --> 00:20:44.160
A very small step towards that, we have been learning
through the development of many AI products,

00:20:44.160 --> 00:20:46.290
especially through our chat bots,

00:20:46.290 --> 00:20:49.680
is really this whole AI thing

00:20:49.680 --> 00:20:53.000
is not just about IQ intelligence.

00:20:53.000 --> 00:20:56.290
It has a lot, what it's all about,
that is about smart.

00:20:56.290 --> 00:20:59.670
It is actually more about empathy
and understanding.

00:20:59.670 --> 00:21:01.920
You know just like humans, like EQ.

00:21:01.920 --> 00:21:05.970
So something that we are still learning,
you know, what does this even mean?

00:21:05.970 --> 00:21:11.800
And when you interact with a human being,
when did you make this kind of recommendation?

00:21:11.800 --> 00:21:12.940
When did you interrupt?

00:21:12.940 --> 00:21:16.180
And a lot of this I think
this is still very, very early.

00:21:16.180 --> 00:21:21.540
My favorite example is actually I was
talking to the XiaoIce team in Beijing,

00:21:24.330 --> 00:21:26.970
and the chat bot knows where this person

00:21:26.970 --> 00:21:29.200
is roughly through IP and others.

00:21:29.770 --> 00:21:33.350
Let's just say that it's already 1:00 a.m.

00:21:33.350 --> 00:21:37.100
in the morning, and this person
keeps talking to the chat bot.

00:21:37.690 --> 00:21:40.670
So should the chat bot slow down the response?

00:21:40.670 --> 00:21:43.530
Should chat bot say, oh, you should go back to sleep?

00:21:43.530 --> 00:21:45.220
What if it's already 2:00 a.m.?

00:21:45.220 --> 00:21:46.860
What if it's actually 3 a.m.?

00:21:48.430 --> 00:21:50.810
Does the chat bot have any responsibility?

00:21:50.810 --> 00:21:54.670
So those things, we just, are very, very early.

00:21:54.670 --> 00:22:00.910
So we just have to really learn along the way,
you know, how to design these kind of AI products.

00:22:00.910 --> 00:22:01.740
- Yes really interesting.

00:22:01.740 --> 00:22:03.000
And of course,

00:22:03.000 --> 00:22:04.770
it needs input from lots
of different disciplines,

00:22:04.770 --> 00:22:07.140
right, not just from
IT technologists.

00:22:08.080 --> 00:22:11.020
You know, social scientists
and many, many others.

00:22:12.050 --> 00:22:12.850
I'm conscious of the time.

00:22:12.850 --> 00:22:14.670
I'm keen to open this up to questions.

00:22:14.670 --> 00:22:17.890
But there's just one more question
I think I'd like to touch on,

00:22:17.890 --> 00:22:20.830
because I know this is of tremendous
interest not just to Microsoft,

00:22:20.830 --> 00:22:23.000
or the whole IT industry,
but to industry generally,

00:22:23.000 --> 00:22:24.890
and of course the academic world.

00:22:24.890 --> 00:22:30.360
Which is this extraordinary explosion
we've seen in demand for talent in this space.

00:22:30.360 --> 00:22:35.220
And what are your thoughts on the
talent pipeline and what we could do?

00:22:35.850 --> 00:22:40.620
- Well this is truly amazing to see
how excited people are about AI.

00:22:40.620 --> 00:22:47.010
You know first of all, then how hungry
the companies are hiring AI talent.

00:22:47.690 --> 00:22:52.750
We all know that the price tag
is just sky high.

00:22:55.320 --> 00:22:59.690
Not too long ago I was at a forum
and I said to the press, I said look.

00:22:59.690 --> 00:23:06.260
Nowadays, if a young kid knows how
to train five layers of Neural Net,

00:23:06.260 --> 00:23:10.020
the kid can absolutely
demand six figures.

00:23:10.020 --> 00:23:15.520
If the kid can train 50 layers of Neural Net,
the kid probably will want seven figures.

00:23:15.520 --> 00:23:20.440
So it's just that the talent market
is very, very hot out there.

00:23:20.440 --> 00:23:25.790
I think this is where we should
work together with academia to train

00:23:25.790 --> 00:23:27.960
the next generation of AI talents,

00:23:27.960 --> 00:23:30.490
so students really know what to do,

00:23:30.490 --> 00:23:33.810
what kind of tools they can do.

00:23:33.810 --> 00:23:37.520
At the same time Microsoft,
companies like Microsoft,

00:23:39.410 --> 00:23:42.670
have responsibilities to come up
with the right tools.

00:23:42.670 --> 00:23:46.630
Like in Microsoft we certainly do,
we actually do the two kinds of tools.

00:23:46.630 --> 00:23:48.330
One is actually for developers.

00:23:48.330 --> 00:23:50.460
They are the ones actually
for data scientists.

00:23:50.460 --> 00:23:56.440
And for data scientists we have Azure machine learning
go all the way, end-to-end, how we train those models

00:23:56.440 --> 00:23:57.970
and to tune those models.

00:23:57.970 --> 00:24:04.800
For developers we have the cognitive service APIs
and we should do even better coming up with Visual

00:24:04.800 --> 00:24:06.290
Studio type of the tools

00:24:06.290 --> 00:24:08.880
so that we can actually help you

00:24:08.880 --> 00:24:12.250
put together a project
and the debugs, the whole process.

00:24:12.250 --> 00:24:17.190
And then compile that, and take all kinds
of framework to compile it together,

00:24:17.190 --> 00:24:19.750
all the way to different
kind of running platforms.

00:24:19.750 --> 00:24:24.920
Like you know, Azure, Azure GPU
or even your home machine.

00:24:26.820 --> 00:24:28.940
So those things I think
we have a responsibility.

00:24:29.620 --> 00:24:34.260
Maybe the last thing I can share with the audience
is that we as a company of course struggle

00:24:35.020 --> 00:24:37.530
to train our own AI talents,

00:24:37.530 --> 00:24:42.920
so much so I decided to start
an AI school several months ago.

00:24:42.920 --> 00:24:45.430
It turns out to be a huge success.

00:24:45.430 --> 00:24:48.050
Everyone wants to learn AI.

00:24:48.050 --> 00:24:54.670
And of course, we want to train every engineer,
developer to become an AI engineer.

00:24:54.670 --> 00:25:00.710
We would love to learn from you
the best practices and even partner together.

00:25:00.710 --> 00:25:02.190
- Great, thanks, Harry.
Fantastic.

00:25:02.190 --> 00:25:03.870
So let's open it up to questions.

00:25:03.870 --> 00:25:06.930
We have various roving mics,
so just put your hand up.

00:25:07.960 --> 00:25:09.630
Somebody will give you a microphone.

00:25:11.240 --> 00:25:13.520
We have somebody here.

00:25:13.520 --> 00:25:14.750
Number 3.

00:25:14.750 --> 00:25:16.980
- Hi, Julie Rockmore,
University of Illinois.

00:25:16.980 --> 00:25:20.550
I was just wondering whether you could expand
a little bit more

00:25:20.550 --> 00:25:22.930
on what you see the role
of academia to be,

00:25:23.950 --> 00:25:26.270
and what we can do as faculty?

00:25:29.370 --> 00:25:31.600
- Well that's a big question.

00:25:33.680 --> 00:25:38.890
The way we look at that is Microsoft Research
has had a very rich history

00:25:38.890 --> 00:25:42.200
of collaborating with many universities

00:25:42.200 --> 00:25:44.850
and many university professors.

00:25:44.850 --> 00:25:51.820
I feel very honored to have many of you choose
to come to visit us at this time

00:25:51.820 --> 00:25:54.000
and coming to our Faculty Summit

00:25:54.000 --> 00:25:56.720
to have a chance so that we can exchange ideas,

00:25:56.720 --> 00:25:58.110
and then we figure out things,

00:25:58.110 --> 00:26:00.750
even more things
that we can work together.

00:26:00.750 --> 00:26:04.990
For Microsoft, and especially for Microsoft
Research, you know we're very open.

00:26:04.990 --> 00:26:09.560
We do a lot of projects
and we write a lot of papers.

00:26:09.560 --> 00:26:14.930
Many of those papers we jointly do
with the professors and the students.

00:26:17.120 --> 00:26:22.210
I don't really have anything that specific,
in general how professors

00:26:22.210 --> 00:26:24.160
should work with us
or work with others.

00:26:24.160 --> 00:26:26.770
I think everyone has a different situation.

00:26:29.320 --> 00:26:30.070
I don't know.

00:26:30.070 --> 00:26:31.220
Chris do you want to add something?

00:26:31.220 --> 00:26:36.690
I know you do things very successfully,
like work with some universities in Europe,

00:26:36.690 --> 00:26:39.040
like the recent announcement
with Amsterdam

00:26:39.040 --> 00:26:41.510
might be a great example,
you can share that.

00:26:41.510 --> 00:26:45.470
- So I think this collaboration
with the academic world is a very deep one

00:26:45.470 --> 00:26:46.680
and incredibly important.

00:26:46.680 --> 00:26:48.570
And I like to think that
it's mutually beneficial.

00:26:49.350 --> 00:26:52.290
As Harry mentioned, just last week
we announced another collaboration,

00:26:52.290 --> 00:26:54.580
this is with Max Welling,
the University of Amsterdam,

00:26:55.160 --> 00:26:56.970
under really sort of
two threads to this.

00:26:56.970 --> 00:26:59.460
One is around this talent pipeline.

00:26:59.460 --> 00:27:01.790
So really trying to partner with academia,

00:27:01.790 --> 00:27:03.280
because it's in everybody's interest

00:27:03.280 --> 00:27:05.240
that we really have a strong talent pipeline

00:27:05.240 --> 00:27:08.200
with the next generation
of AI researchers coming along.

00:27:08.200 --> 00:27:12.470
So one of the things we do with Amsterdam,
but actually more generally across Europe,

00:27:12.470 --> 00:27:16.460
is we fund actually a very large
number PhD students,

00:27:16.460 --> 00:27:17.970
but we don't just
fund the students.

00:27:17.970 --> 00:27:20.830
I think more importantly, we are actually
almost all of those students

00:27:20.830 --> 00:27:24.170
are co-supervised
by researchers in MSR.

00:27:24.170 --> 00:27:27.160
And so it's a way of sort of adding
some intellectual horsepower

00:27:27.160 --> 00:27:29.870
that augments that available
in the universities,

00:27:29.870 --> 00:27:31.680
and also helps the students
get a little bit

00:27:31.680 --> 00:27:33.670
of more of an industrial perspective

00:27:33.670 --> 00:27:36.110
as well to complement
the academic perspective.

00:27:36.110 --> 00:27:37.690
And then, so that's one strand.

00:27:37.690 --> 00:27:39.500
And then the other strand
is on the research side,

00:27:39.500 --> 00:27:42.800
where we're looking at some of the really
cutting edge, the futuristic,

00:27:42.800 --> 00:27:44.570
the very long-term
aspects of things

00:27:44.570 --> 00:27:46.510
around machine learning in AI.

00:27:46.510 --> 00:27:51.170
Where we see really complementary strengths
in the case the University of Amsterdam,

00:27:52.030 --> 00:27:55.150
tremendous strengths in areas
around unsupervised learning

00:27:55.150 --> 00:27:57.310
and rich probabilistic
models combining probabilistic

00:27:57.310 --> 00:27:58.750
methods with deep learning.

00:27:58.750 --> 00:28:03.410
Looking at things like causal
discovery from data and sure, we have expertise in

00:28:03.410 --> 00:28:04.890
MSR in those areas.

00:28:04.890 --> 00:28:07.640
But you know, this is a vast
and very active field

00:28:07.640 --> 00:28:09.550
and we can't do
everything on our own.

00:28:09.550 --> 00:28:12.030
And so these partnerships
are incredibly beneficial.

00:28:12.030 --> 00:28:14.650
So I think those two things,
both the talent pipeline

00:28:14.650 --> 00:28:16.870
and also the research collaboration

00:28:16.870 --> 00:28:21.980
are just incredibly
important to us.

00:28:21.980 --> 00:28:23.330
Who's next?

00:28:23.330 --> 00:28:24.110
OK.

00:28:24.110 --> 00:28:25.390
Yes.
Number two.

00:28:25.390 --> 00:28:28.310
If you just give us your name and affiliation
as well, that would be fantastic, thanks.

00:28:28.840 --> 00:28:30.980
- Hi, I'm Barbara
Grosz from Harvard.

00:28:30.980 --> 00:28:35.780
So you can tell by my gray hair that in a few decades
I'm going to retire.

00:28:35.780 --> 00:28:40.050
And the advice for people retiring
is to diversify their portfolio.

00:28:40.050 --> 00:28:44.520
So I'm seeing that you're making
a really heavy investment in deep learning.

00:28:44.520 --> 00:28:48.610
You can also tell from my gray hair
that I've been an AI for even more decades

00:28:48.610 --> 00:28:51.390
than MSR has existed.

00:28:51.390 --> 00:28:56.800
And I I've seen these trends where
there's one technique that's everything.

00:28:56.800 --> 00:28:59.150
And deep learning is doing great things.

00:28:59.150 --> 00:29:03.760
But I'm wondering how diversified
do you see your portfolio of AI being,

00:29:03.760 --> 00:29:06.580
both in terms of the research
and in terms of your school?

00:29:07.180 --> 00:29:08.780
- Well listen to my talk tomorrow.

00:29:08.780 --> 00:29:09.750
That's part of the answer.

00:29:09.750 --> 00:29:12.540
I'll let Harry
- Why don't you finish your answer first,

00:29:12.540 --> 00:29:14.310
and then I will
complement yours, yeah.

00:29:14.310 --> 00:29:19.900
- Yeah, so you know, deep learning
has been undoubtedly a breakthrough.

00:29:19.900 --> 00:29:23.750
So there are things that we now do
with much higher levels of performance

00:29:23.750 --> 00:29:25.070
than we did before.

00:29:25.070 --> 00:29:29.280
I think probably that breakthrough in deep learning
has really rejuvenated the whole field,

00:29:29.280 --> 00:29:32.740
and in some sense, the reason we're excited
about AI is because of that.

00:29:32.740 --> 00:29:37.530
However, the idea that everything can be solved
by just shoveling label data through a deep neural

00:29:37.530 --> 00:29:39.310
network will of course
be completely naive.

00:29:40.180 --> 00:29:44.660
So I think it injects a lot of energy
and enthusiasm into the field which is fantastic.

00:29:44.660 --> 00:29:50.800
I also think that it offers a new building block
that we can combine with other techniques,

00:29:50.800 --> 00:29:52.380
and that can amplify
those other techniques

00:29:52.380 --> 00:29:54.300
and can advance the whole field.

00:29:54.300 --> 00:29:58.780
But it's certainly not on its own a complete solution
to all the things we want to achieve.

00:29:58.780 --> 00:30:03.090
So yes, I'll be giving a talk tomorrow,
a keynote in which I'll talk about

00:30:03.670 --> 00:30:05.950
a somewhat broader perspective
on machine learning

00:30:05.950 --> 00:30:07.900
that hopefully will help
answer the question.

00:30:08.830 --> 00:30:10.150
- I add a little bit.

00:30:10.150 --> 00:30:12.840
Well first of all, Barbara,
welcome to Microsoft again.

00:30:12.840 --> 00:30:13.610
You know we love you.

00:30:13.610 --> 00:30:15.410
You should come to visit us more often.

00:30:15.410 --> 00:30:20.160
And Eric always talks about you,
and to send more students to us.

00:30:24.520 --> 00:30:30.410
I really think that, you know, we first of all should
thank deep learning for it's incredible contribution

00:30:30.410 --> 00:30:32.590
and the impact
in the field of AI.

00:30:32.590 --> 00:30:35.890
There's a lot of excitement
because of that deep learning.

00:30:35.890 --> 00:30:42.350
In certain areas it actually worked beautifully
in speech recognition and vision recognition

00:30:42.350 --> 00:30:45.380
and other things like
deep reinforcement learning.

00:30:46.160 --> 00:30:51.800
I don't think people are naive thinking that
deep learning is going to solve every problem,

00:30:51.800 --> 00:30:54.620
and I don't think people
feel that way.

00:30:55.440 --> 00:31:01.240
But until we actually can build the tools
that incorporate a lot of interesting

00:31:01.240 --> 00:31:02.860
deep learning models to a way

00:31:02.860 --> 00:31:05.400
that millions
and millions of developers,

00:31:05.400 --> 00:31:07.240
engineers can easily do that,

00:31:07.240 --> 00:31:10.260
it is just like solving second order
in the differential equation,

00:31:10.260 --> 00:31:12.360
or whatever.

00:31:12.360 --> 00:31:15.910
I think that until then,
people will still be mystified.

00:31:15.910 --> 00:31:20.100
You know, what this deep learning really is,
and what's really going on?

00:31:20.100 --> 00:31:25.590
I hope that we, in the next few years,
will be able to contribute

00:31:25.590 --> 00:31:28.350
with great tools coming from Microsoft.

00:31:28.930 --> 00:31:32.850
If you really look at
what's happening in deep learning,

00:31:33.390 --> 00:31:35.820
a lot of those efforts
has been really

00:31:35.820 --> 00:31:39.240
on the kind of architectural design
of the deep neural nets

00:31:39.240 --> 00:31:41.820
that actually can perform
better than others.

00:31:42.640 --> 00:31:48.380
And then you think about really deep
intellectual contributions coming out of the field.

00:31:50.670 --> 00:31:57.030
They do have something like, you know,
generative adversarial networks and a few things like that.

00:31:57.030 --> 00:31:58.560
I'd love to see even more.

00:31:58.560 --> 00:32:03.960
I think maybe we should do more deep learning research
if we see more coming out of that.

00:32:03.960 --> 00:32:06.410
But still you have to go back
to those fundamentals.

00:32:06.410 --> 00:32:09.000
Many of those things
that Eric talked about.

00:32:09.000 --> 00:32:17.240
We certainly diversify in MSR AI the areas
that we focus on, get to the deep understanding

00:32:17.240 --> 00:32:18.980
of what is intelligence?

00:32:18.980 --> 00:32:20.220
What is cognition?

00:32:20.220 --> 00:32:22.470
How did you really build the models?

00:32:22.470 --> 00:32:24.660
So that's what we do here.

00:32:25.970 --> 00:32:27.970
- Great.
Fantastic.

00:32:28.540 --> 00:32:30.960
Who's next?

00:32:30.960 --> 00:32:31.930
- In the back.

00:32:31.930 --> 00:32:34.520
- Right in the far corner in the back is somebody.

00:32:37.090 --> 00:32:39.090
OK.
Number three.

00:32:39.630 --> 00:32:40.940
- Hi, David Karger, MIT.

00:32:42.760 --> 00:32:49.280
So what's been striking me today
in a lot of the presentations is how a lot of the work

00:32:49.280 --> 00:32:53.430
that's going on in AI
is about getting computers to do things

00:32:54.820 --> 00:32:57.860
like reading or having a conversation

00:32:57.860 --> 00:33:00.190
that relatively untrained humans

00:33:00.190 --> 00:33:02.190
are actually quite good at.

00:33:03.020 --> 00:33:08.720
And I wonder if you could talk a little bit about,
I mean, we know that the downsides

00:33:08.720 --> 00:33:11.460
of that for the future
of work and things like that,

00:33:11.460 --> 00:33:13.320
but I wonder if you could
talk a little about ways

00:33:13.320 --> 00:33:16.580
that AI might complement humans

00:33:16.580 --> 00:33:19.160
and empower them to do things
that they can't do,

00:33:19.160 --> 00:33:21.610
as opposed to replacing things
that they're already pretty good at?

00:33:24.230 --> 00:33:27.000
- Maybe I will start it,
and then Chris, you can add more.

00:33:29.780 --> 00:33:31.350
So thank you very much, David.

00:33:34.080 --> 00:33:37.290
I hate to use this marketing slogan,
but that's the marketing slogan

00:33:37.290 --> 00:33:38.650
we use at Microsoft

00:33:38.650 --> 00:33:40.990
when we talk about the Microsoft AI.

00:33:40.990 --> 00:33:46.060
We say Microsoft AI is all about
amplifying human ingenuity.

00:33:46.650 --> 00:33:52.140
It's really about adding more value
to humans.

00:33:52.140 --> 00:33:56.360
We talk about, it's all about the reason
we actually do Microsoft AI

00:33:56.360 --> 00:33:58.700
is about the man and machine partnership,

00:33:58.700 --> 00:34:01.180
something that Chris,

00:34:01.180 --> 00:34:03.160
Eric and others from

00:34:03.160 --> 00:34:06.480
MSR have helped our CEO,

00:34:06.480 --> 00:34:07.890
our senior leadership team,

00:34:07.890 --> 00:34:09.960
to really frame what we really do there.

00:34:10.590 --> 00:34:15.180
I think there are numerous examples
where that AI adds value there.

00:34:15.180 --> 00:34:18.530
I'll give you a very trivial example that, you know,
I just showed this morning

00:34:18.530 --> 00:34:22.160
at our internal event
this morning out in Vegas,

00:34:22.160 --> 00:34:24.600
is actually this intelligent camera app.

00:34:26.130 --> 00:34:31.190
So it was something we actually released
about a year ago, it is called the Microsoft Pix,

00:34:32.170 --> 00:34:34.100
it's an intelligent camera app.

00:34:35.220 --> 00:34:37.690
It has a lot of interesting
technologies there.

00:34:37.690 --> 00:34:42.890
In fact, we use the 20 some papers in the company
from MSR, and I put all those text there.

00:34:42.890 --> 00:34:46.850
But I'll give you one example
that is actually if you use this app,

00:34:46.850 --> 00:34:48.670
you take a picture of anyone,

00:34:48.670 --> 00:34:51.170
I guarantee you your eyes will be open.

00:34:54.830 --> 00:34:56.440
So is this a reasonable example?

00:34:56.440 --> 00:35:04.200
So how do you guarantee when you take a picture
of someone that this person's eyes are always open?

00:35:04.200 --> 00:35:08.990
And it turns out that we have already had
this kind of computer vision,

00:35:08.990 --> 00:35:10.940
machine learning techniques there.

00:35:10.940 --> 00:35:14.140
The way you do that is you
really take the longer sequence,

00:35:14.140 --> 00:35:16.470
typically we take 10 frames,

00:35:16.470 --> 00:35:21.170
and the chances are,
you are not going to blink that long.

00:35:25.580 --> 00:35:29.750
- That's actually a perfect example
of replacing what a human can do.

00:35:29.750 --> 00:35:32.460
- A human, if you take 10 pictures,
then a human can go through it

00:35:32.460 --> 00:35:34.460
and find one with the eyes open.

00:35:35.000 --> 00:35:36.080
- Well I think it's debatable.

00:35:36.080 --> 00:35:37.660
I think Chris, you should add something

00:35:37.660 --> 00:35:40.710
you have been thinking about
in a man-machine partnership,

00:35:40.710 --> 00:35:43.060
and how we think about this at Microsoft.

00:35:43.060 --> 00:35:45.030
- Sure.
I think first of all this idea

00:35:45.030 --> 00:35:47.420
of artificial intelligence being a field

00:35:47.420 --> 00:35:50.200
in which you list
all the capabilities of humans,

00:35:50.200 --> 00:35:51.770
and then you cross them off one at a time,

00:35:51.770 --> 00:35:55.480
achieve X and Y and Z, very dystopian, depressing.

00:35:55.480 --> 00:35:57.010
What happens when you get
to the bottom of the list?

00:35:57.010 --> 00:35:59.400
You know, that's very unappealing.

00:35:59.400 --> 00:36:05.010
The other general thought is that it's true today,
and it's going to be true for at least

00:36:05.010 --> 00:36:06.830
as far ahead
as I can foresee,

00:36:06.830 --> 00:36:08.710
that the capabilities of machines

00:36:08.710 --> 00:36:11.650
are very different and complementary
to the capabilities of people.

00:36:11.650 --> 00:36:14.430
And we know that when you
combine things with different,

00:36:14.430 --> 00:36:16.690
you know, when you combine
two classifiers together

00:36:16.690 --> 00:36:17.990
with de-correlated areas,

00:36:17.990 --> 00:36:19.570
the performance of the two together

00:36:19.570 --> 00:36:21.260
exceeds that of either alone.

00:36:21.260 --> 00:36:25.550
If you look at chess
playing machines, they can beat humans,

00:36:25.550 --> 00:36:27.010
but actually the human and machine

00:36:27.010 --> 00:36:29.540
together can beat any human or any machine,

00:36:29.540 --> 00:36:31.650
the Centaur model.

00:36:31.650 --> 00:36:32.940
I think that's true very generally.

00:36:32.940 --> 00:36:36.750
So to give you a very concrete and sort of very
near-term example of a project

00:36:36.750 --> 00:36:39.050
which was actually
in the tech showcase

00:36:40.230 --> 00:36:41.500
is the project
called Inner Eyes.

00:36:41.500 --> 00:36:45.000
So there what we're doing
is helping radiation oncologists

00:36:45.000 --> 00:36:46.260
do radiation therapy planning.

00:36:46.260 --> 00:36:50.120
That's a big deal,
because half of all of us

00:36:50.120 --> 00:36:52.990
are going to end up
with some form of cancer during our lives.

00:36:52.990 --> 00:36:55.590
- That's depressing.
- That's kind of depressing, and of those,

00:36:55.590 --> 00:36:57.750
half of those are going
to need radiation therapy.

00:36:57.750 --> 00:36:59.150
So it's a very big deal.

00:36:59.790 --> 00:37:04.220
But to have radiation therapy,
you fire radiation in from multiple angles.

00:37:04.220 --> 00:37:07.210
The idea is to dump as much
energy into the tumor as possible,

00:37:07.210 --> 00:37:09.240
as little energy
into good tissue as possible,

00:37:09.240 --> 00:37:10.810
and especially
into vital organs.

00:37:10.810 --> 00:37:13.280
So you need a three-dimensional
segmentation of the tumor

00:37:13.280 --> 00:37:15.730
and a three-dimensional segmentation
of your vital organs,

00:37:15.730 --> 00:37:17.170
eyes and whatever else
there might be.

00:37:18.190 --> 00:37:23.540
That's something which is currently done
manually by oncologists looking through

00:37:23.540 --> 00:37:26.540
MRI scans, slice by slice,
and segmenting them.

00:37:26.540 --> 00:37:28.260
It's tedious, it's slow.

00:37:28.260 --> 00:37:31.640
The oncologist gets kind of tired
and it's not so reproducible.

00:37:31.640 --> 00:37:37.860
It's screaming at for using machine learning,
that's exactly what the Inner Eye project does.

00:37:37.860 --> 00:37:40.170
It's not trying to replace
the oncologist, it's trying to say look.

00:37:40.170 --> 00:37:41.270
Here's a segmentation.

00:37:41.270 --> 00:37:44.900
Instead of taking half an hour,
an hour, three hours in some cases,

00:37:44.900 --> 00:37:47.460
in a few seconds it
gives the oncologist segmentation.

00:37:47.460 --> 00:37:50.440
They can add then their expertise
and say actually, you know,

00:37:50.440 --> 00:37:52.210
it's not bad but actually I can refine it

00:37:52.210 --> 00:37:54.380
and tweak it a little bit,
using their experience

00:37:54.380 --> 00:37:56.720
and in a few minutes
they've got a good segmentation.

00:37:56.720 --> 00:37:59.750
And it frees up their time to be
doing things they should be doing,

00:37:59.750 --> 00:38:02.930
which is thinking more qualitatively
about the whole tear pathway,

00:38:02.930 --> 00:38:04.630
interacting with the patient.

00:38:04.630 --> 00:38:07.600
And I don't know how things are
in the U.S., probably fairly similar.

00:38:07.600 --> 00:38:08.150
In the U.K.

00:38:08.150 --> 00:38:11.490
we're facing a shortfall
of around 4,000 radiologists,

00:38:11.490 --> 00:38:13.590
and so we're really screaming
out for technologies

00:38:13.590 --> 00:38:15.900
which can empower
those radiologists

00:38:15.900 --> 00:38:17.970
to make them more efficient
and more effective.

00:38:17.970 --> 00:38:20.810
Effectively, freeing up the stuff
that the machines are good at,

00:38:20.810 --> 00:38:23.360
and leaving people to do the things
that they're good at.

00:38:23.360 --> 00:38:28.970
So I see this complementarity
as intellectually much more appealing,

00:38:28.970 --> 00:38:30.770
but also actually something
of an imperative.

00:38:33.710 --> 00:38:35.680
Shall we just, we're kind of
running out of time.

00:38:35.680 --> 00:38:37.490
Let's just take
one more question.

00:38:38.670 --> 00:38:39.940
You get to the microphone first.

00:38:39.940 --> 00:38:40.900
Number two.
OK.

00:38:40.900 --> 00:38:46.660
Can you give us your name and affiliation?

00:38:46.660 --> 00:38:48.280
- Pedro Domingos,
University of Washington.

00:38:48.890 --> 00:38:51.930
So if you look at what's happened
with technology and business

00:38:51.930 --> 00:38:53.670
in the last couple
of hundred years,

00:38:53.670 --> 00:38:56.220
every time a major new
technology comes along,

00:38:56.220 --> 00:38:58.820
it leads to a new kind
of dominant business.

00:38:58.820 --> 00:39:01.970
So you know, like in the second half
of the 19th century it was the railways,

00:39:01.970 --> 00:39:05.470
and then, you know,
when Ford invented the assembly line,

00:39:05.470 --> 00:39:06.840
that was a whole new kind of,

00:39:07.580 --> 00:39:08.850
multinationals were the product

00:39:08.850 --> 00:39:11.360
of telecoms and air travel and whatnot.

00:39:11.360 --> 00:39:14.480
And AI surely is one
such major new technology.

00:39:14.480 --> 00:39:19.680
So the question is, what is that business,
what is the new kind of business

00:39:19.680 --> 00:39:21.730
that is going
to result from AI?

00:39:21.730 --> 00:39:25.050
How is it going to be different
from the businesses that exist today,

00:39:25.050 --> 00:39:27.050
and how do we get there?

00:39:28.310 --> 00:39:31.270
- Well I can give it a try.

00:39:31.270 --> 00:39:36.210
At least to share some of the things
that we have been thinking about, Pedro.

00:39:39.300 --> 00:39:44.650
One key thing along this line of AI,
creating all the business,

00:39:47.020 --> 00:39:48.700
until now people do believe

00:39:48.700 --> 00:39:51.400
that the magic is in the data.

00:39:52.260 --> 00:39:55.480
So you have to have data
to make interesting things.

00:39:55.480 --> 00:39:57.830
That could change in the future.

00:39:57.830 --> 00:39:59.490
But this is where we see that.

00:39:59.490 --> 00:40:01.160
So people do worry about that.

00:40:03.040 --> 00:40:06.500
Does this mean that only the big five,
you know the top companies

00:40:06.500 --> 00:40:08.740
actually have the opportunities?

00:40:08.740 --> 00:40:10.990
The rich get richer,
and all this kind of thing.

00:40:12.540 --> 00:40:16.310
At Microsoft, especially in MSR,
we have been thinking about this thing.

00:40:16.310 --> 00:40:20.190
It was together with some economists
that we have been thinking about this.

00:40:20.190 --> 00:40:26.110
And what particular angle we are actually thinking
about is what do we call the data as labor?

00:40:26.940 --> 00:40:32.810
So that might be one very interesting way
to think about the future as a society,

00:40:32.810 --> 00:40:34.960
as the economy, the business model.

00:40:34.960 --> 00:40:37.250
You know, what does this
really even mean?

00:40:37.250 --> 00:40:40.440
Because if you think about that,
especially and I was educated

00:40:40.440 --> 00:40:42.390
by my colleague
Jaron Lanier,

00:40:43.190 --> 00:40:44.860
you know if this whole ML thing

00:40:44.860 --> 00:40:47.930
is going to really dominate,
would that be doomsday?

00:40:47.930 --> 00:40:49.790
There's nothing
for humans to do.

00:40:49.790 --> 00:40:52.160
You know, so we don't
believe that is the case.

00:40:52.160 --> 00:40:58.910
Or, if you really think about, there
could be that the humans can continue to contribute,

00:40:58.910 --> 00:41:03.280
especially the lower part
of the society income-wise.

00:41:03.280 --> 00:41:06.660
You know, what can they really
contribute to society?

00:41:06.660 --> 00:41:10.050
What should be the future
work for those people?

00:41:10.050 --> 00:41:15.290
So I think that's one particular direction
that we actually think about it.

00:41:15.290 --> 00:41:19.110
But it doesn't really mean just say,
you know, we're coming up with a new company

00:41:19.110 --> 00:41:20.950
with one particular business model,

00:41:20.950 --> 00:41:23.710
monopolize everything and making
most of the money.

00:41:23.710 --> 00:41:25.780
It's actually more thinking
for the whole society.

00:41:27.350 --> 00:41:28.460
- Great.
Thank you very much.

00:41:28.460 --> 00:41:30.830
Well we really are definitely
well out of time there.

00:41:30.830 --> 00:41:34.090
So I know there are more questions,
but I think we should probably wrap there

00:41:34.090 --> 00:41:36.300
so we can all get off
and enjoy a nice dinner.

00:41:36.300 --> 00:41:41.480
But many, many interesting questions
and whatever happens over the next few years

00:41:41.480 --> 00:41:43.220
is going to be an extremely interesting time

00:41:43.220 --> 00:41:44.770
for the field of AI.

00:41:44.770 --> 00:41:47.320
I'm going to invite Sandy back
to make a few closing comments.

00:41:47.320 --> 00:41:51.540
Before I do, could I just ask you to join me
in thanking Harry for a fascinating discussion.

00:41:51.540 --> 00:41:54.820
- Thanks.

